{"ex-403-1":{"number":"1","title":"Short circuit","question":"<p>In the circuit shown, how do the readings of the ammeter and the voltmeter change when the switch is closed? Explain.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/9db938c5123db3bd224ca636.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:193.9px\"/></p>","answer":"<p>When the switch is closed, bulb <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> is shorted.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/b9a13534f1407cfca49c9f51.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:457.3px\"/></p><p>The total resistance of the circuit decreases and the current through the circuit increases. Therefore, the ammeter reading increases.</p><p>The voltmeter is now connected across a branch of almost zero resistance, so its reading decreases.</p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 4  |  Chapter 403  |  Source PDF p. 3","source":{"name":"403 Examples.docx","sha256":"d0f30b6f4b9417571c9d71444729227ec94d5a61ffb3ccd4ed12515a0eb9e196","block":2},"id":"ex-403-1","chapter":"403"},"ex-403-2":{"number":"2","title":"Potential divider","question":"<p>A uniform resistance wire <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>Q</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> is connected to a 1.5-V battery. It is 30 cm long and has a total resistance of 120 Ω. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>S</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> is a sliding contact which can move along <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>Q</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>. Initially <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>S</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> is 20 cm long.</p><p>(a) Find the voltage <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> across <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p><p>(b) Then a light bulb is connected across</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/db552c19f773e4963a271668.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:195.9px\"/></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>. How will the brightness of the bulb change when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>S</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> is moved towards <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Q</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>?</p>","answer":"<p>(a) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>S</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>Q</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>Q</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> × ε = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>–</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> × 1.5 = 0.5 V</p><p>(b) When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>S</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> is moved towards <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Q</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>, the voltage across the bulb decreases. Therefore, the bulb becomes dimmer.</p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 4  |  Chapter 403  |  Source PDF p. 5","source":{"name":"403 Examples.docx","sha256":"d0f30b6f4b9417571c9d71444729227ec94d5a61ffb3ccd4ed12515a0eb9e196","block":11},"id":"ex-403-2","chapter":"403"},"ex-403-3":{"number":"3","title":"Complicated network","question":"<p>Some resistors are connected as shown. Find the equivalent resistance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>q</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> of the network across <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/8205faaf02c2537884159a5f.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:134.3px\"/></p>","answer":"<p>The network is equivalent to the one as shown.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>q</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>′ = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>+</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>+</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>+</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 5 Ω</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>q</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>+</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>+</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>+</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 4 Ω</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/2c04926fa4846098cc2a6c82.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:301.3px\"/></p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 4  |  Chapter 403  |  Source PDF p. 7","source":{"name":"403 Examples.docx","sha256":"d0f30b6f4b9417571c9d71444729227ec94d5a61ffb3ccd4ed12515a0eb9e196","block":21},"id":"ex-403-3","chapter":"403"},"ex-403-4":{"number":"4","title":"Variable resistor and resistor network","question":"<p>In the figure, variable resistor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> is connected with four identical resistors <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>C</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>D</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>. When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> is set to 24 Ω, the equivalent resistance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> across <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> is 15 Ω. Find <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is 8 Ω.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/2c623b8b506c3c2678ca5bd9.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:196.5px\"/></p>","answer":"<p>Resistors <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>D</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> can be replaced by a resistor of resistance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>q</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>.</p><p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 24 Ω,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>q</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>q</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = 40 Ω</p><p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = 8 Ω,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 10 Ω</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/2247bb000db6462924c7f908.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:338.0px\"/></p><p>Revision 3 Q15 (p.138)</p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p>You need not calculate the resistance of each resistor.</p>","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 4  |  Chapter 403  |  Source PDF p. 8","source":{"name":"403 Examples.docx","sha256":"d0f30b6f4b9417571c9d71444729227ec94d5a61ffb3ccd4ed12515a0eb9e196","block":30},"id":"ex-403-4","chapter":"403"},"ex-403-5":{"number":"5","title":"Effect of internal resistance of voltmeter","question":"<p>Consider the circuit shown.</p><p>Find the reading of the voltmeter if</p><p>(a) it is ideal;</p><p>(b) its internal resistance is 30 Ω;</p><p>(c) its internal resistance is 1000 Ω.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/eedb71ab804441a831a36532.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:170.2px\"/></p>","answer":"<p>(a) Reading of voltmeter = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>+</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> × 1.5 = 1 V</p><p>(b) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>q</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> of 10-Ω resistor and voltmeter = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>+</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 7.5 Ω</p><p>Reading of voltmeter = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>7</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>7</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>+</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> × 1.5 = 0.9 V</p><p>(c) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>q</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> of 10-Ω resistor and voltmeter = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>+</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 9.90 Ω</p><p>Reading of voltmeter = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>+</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> × 1.5 = 0.997 V</p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 4  |  Chapter 403  |  Source PDF p. 13","source":{"name":"403 Examples.docx","sha256":"d0f30b6f4b9417571c9d71444729227ec94d5a61ffb3ccd4ed12515a0eb9e196","block":47},"id":"ex-403-5","chapter":"403"},"ex-403-6":{"number":"6","title":"Network with ideal ammeter and voltmeter","question":"<p>Light bulbs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> have the same resistance of 2 Ω. What are the readings of the ammeter and voltmeter in the following circuits? The ammeter and voltmeter are ideal.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/12db512f65d302910bfe7cc9.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:448.8px\"/></p>","answer":"<p>(a)</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/df248e2f1e602362af82207f.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:237.7px\"/></p><p>Voltmeter reading = ε = 3 V</p><p>Ammeter reading = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 1.5 A</p><p>(b)</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/a85c256c05458bf9e9212b2b.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:83.1px\"/></p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/1da7d30b7ff82a7aea03db44.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:154.1px\"/></p><p>Voltmeter reading = ε = 3 V</p><p>Ammeter reading = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 1.5 A</p><p>Revision 3 Q4 (p.136)</p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p>Y is the only load in the circuit.</p><p><b>Tip</b></p><p>X and Y are connected in parallel to the battery.</p>","think":"<p>What is the current through the battery in (a) and (b)?</p>","thinkAnswer":"<p>(a) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>. The ideal voltmeter draws no current, and Y is the only load.</p><p>(b) X and Y are in parallel. Each draws <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>, so the battery current is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>+</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p>","location":"Book 4  |  Chapter 403  |  Source PDF p. 14","source":{"name":"403 Examples.docx","sha256":"d0f30b6f4b9417571c9d71444729227ec94d5a61ffb3ccd4ed12515a0eb9e196","block":61},"id":"ex-403-6","chapter":"403"},"ex-403-7":{"number":"7","title":"Voltmeter-ammeter method","question":"<p>In the circuit shown, an ammeter of resistance 5 Ω and a voltmeter of resistance 10 kΩ are used to determine the resistance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> of a resistor.</p><p>(a) What does the ammeter measure?</p><p>(b) What does the voltmeter measure?</p><p>(c) Find the percentage error in the value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> obtained using this circuit if</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/2d29bca034d16b336dcd1cdc.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:156.4px\"/></p><p>(i) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 2 Ω;</p><p>(ii) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 2 kΩ.</p>","answer":"<p>(a) It measures the total current through <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and the voltmeter.</p><p>(b) It measures the voltage across <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p><p>(c) (i) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>q</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> of voltmeter and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>+</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>+</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 1.9996 Ω</p><p>Total resistance of circuit = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>q</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = 5 + 1.9996 = 6.9996 Ω</p><p>Ammeter reading <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>ε</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>+</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>q</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 0.857 19 A</p><p>Voltmeter reading <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>q</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>+</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>q</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> × ε = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> × 6 = 1.7140 V</p><p>Measured value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>7</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>7</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 1.9996 Ω</p><p>Percentage error = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>–</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> × 100%</p><p>= –0.0222%</p><p>(ii) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>q</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> of voltmeter and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>+</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>+</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 1666.7 Ω</p><p>Total resistance of circuit = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>q</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = 5 + 1666.7 = 1671.7 Ω</p><p>Ammeter reading = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>ε</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>+</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>q</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>7</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>7</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 3.5892 × 10<sup>–3</sup> A</p><p>Voltmeter reading = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>q</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>+</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>q</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> × ε = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>7</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>7</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>7</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> × 6 = 5.9821 V</p><p>Measured value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>–</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 1666.7 Ω</p><p>Percentage error = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>7</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>−</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> × 100% = –16.7%</p><p>Exercise 3.2 Q5 (p.133)</p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p>Measured value of r</p><p>= <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></p><p>= <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>q</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>+</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>ε</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>ε</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>q</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>+</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>q</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></p><p>= R<sub>eq</sub></p>","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 4  |  Chapter 403  |  Source PDF p. 16","source":{"name":"403 Examples.docx","sha256":"d0f30b6f4b9417571c9d71444729227ec94d5a61ffb3ccd4ed12515a0eb9e196","block":86},"id":"ex-403-7","chapter":"403"},"ex-403-8":{"number":"8","title":"Experiment on internal resistance of battery","question":"<p>Willy uses the circuit below (Fig a) to find the internal resistance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> of a battery of e.m.f. ε. After closing the switch, he varies the resistance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> of the variable resistor and records the corresponding voltmeter reading <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and ammeter reading <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>. The <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>–<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> graph obtained is as shown (Fig b). Assume that the ammeter and the voltmeter are ideal.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/0b3f09e845f2e0624e2cb76f.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:424.4px\"/></p><p>(a) Express <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> in terms of ε, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p><p>(b) Find ε and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p><p>(c) State a precaution for this experiment.</p><p>(d) What is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> when (i) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 2 V; (ii) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 0?</p>","answer":"<p>(a) ε = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> ⇒ <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = (–<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>)<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> + ε</p><p>(b) ε = 3 V</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = – slope = – <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>−</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>−</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 0.5 Ω</p><p>(c) Set the resistance of the variable resistor to its maximum value before starting the experiment. OR</p><p>Open the switch immediately after taking each set of readings.</p><p>(d) (i) From Figure b, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 2 V ⇒ <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 2 A</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 1 Ω</p><p>(ii) By <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>, since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> ≠ 0, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 0 ⇒ <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 0</p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p>V = (– r)<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> + ε</p><p>y-intercept of V–<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> graph = ε</p><p>Slope of V–<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> graph = – r</p>","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 4  |  Chapter 403  |  Source PDF p. 19","source":{"name":"403 Examples.docx","sha256":"d0f30b6f4b9417571c9d71444729227ec94d5a61ffb3ccd4ed12515a0eb9e196","block":118},"id":"ex-403-8","chapter":"403"},"ex-403-9":{"number":"9","title":"Practical battery and ideal battery","question":"<p>In the circuit, the battery has an e.m.f. ε of 3 V and internal resistance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>. The current through <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> is 0.9 A.</p><p>(a) Find the terminal voltage <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p><p>(b) Find <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p><p>(c) How will the current through bulb <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> change if bulb <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> burns out?</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/55d1838476859b7515ce1ffa.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:134.0px\"/></p><p>(d) What will the answer to (c) be if the battery is ideal?</p>","answer":"<p>(a) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = 0.9 × 2 = 1.8 V</p><p>(b) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 0.6 A</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = 0.9 + 0.6 = 1.5 A</p><p>By ε = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>,</p><p>3 = 1.8 + (1.5)<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 0.8 Ω</p><p>(c) Total resistance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> of circuit = 2 + 0.8 = 2.8 Ω</p><p>Current through <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>ε</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 1.07 A &gt; 0.9 A</p><p>∴ The current increases.</p><p>(d) The current through <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> will remain unchanged.</p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p>X and Y are connected in parallel to an ideal battery.</p><p>V<sub>X</sub> = ε and is not affected by the parallel branch containing Y.</p>","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 4  |  Chapter 403  |  Source PDF p. 20","source":{"name":"403 Examples.docx","sha256":"d0f30b6f4b9417571c9d71444729227ec94d5a61ffb3ccd4ed12515a0eb9e196","block":140},"id":"ex-403-9","chapter":"403"}}